form does not express the purpose of stopping.","D":"**Stopped to prepare** gives the purpose of pausing the walk."}}},{"id":"9c9eae86-f31c-54ed-a044-bb186f039f01","topicSlug":"verb-pattern-meaning","level":"B2","prompt":"The task is still ahead. Complete the reminder: “Please remember ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Locate the task in time: it is a **future duty**, not a memory.","explanation":"**Remember to repair** means remember the duty before carrying it out.","keywords":["remember-future-duty"],"skill":"remember-future-duty","contextKey":"verb-pattern-meaning-50","fingerprint":"b8fd339aa75fb4d08b457dc8db525d6e38a40ae10d39bd3d4063838e286314b1","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verb-meaning"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Locate the task in time: it is a **future duty**, not a memory.","**Remember to repair** means remember the duty before carrying it out.","Choose **to repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Remember + -ing recalls an earlier experience; the task is still ahead.","B":"Remember normally needs a to-infinitive for this duty meaning.","C":"A past form does not supply the required complement.","D":"**Remember to repair** means remember the duty before carrying it out."}}},{"id":"e4ccb260-14b6-5bc3-a856-e12a085d1719","topicSlug":"verb-pattern-meaning","level":"B2","prompt":"The task happened yesterday and I recall doing it: “I remember ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The speaker is **recalling an experience that already happened**.","explanation":"**Remember repairing** recalls the earlier experience explicitly described.","keywords":["remember-past-experience"],"skill":"remember-past-experience","contextKey":"verb-pattern-meaning-50","fingerprint":"4c4b5ac06e6fcaad857a196acb179bcd6c62d4715e7d414e33ed95d09dcfeb4b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verb-meaning"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The speaker is **recalling an experience that already happened**.","**Remember repairing** recalls the earlier experience explicitly described.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A base verb without to does not form this complement.","B":"The past form is not the required -ing complement.","C":"**Remember repairing** recalls the earlier experience explicitly described.","D":"Remember to points to remembering a duty before doing it, rather than recalling the experience now."}}},{"id":"fb4129c1-3869-5928-afeb-95df988ab1a0","topicSlug":"verb-pattern-meaning","level":"B2","prompt":"They ceased this activity completely: “They stopped ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The activity itself **ended**; this is stop + -ing.","explanation":"**Stopped repairing** means they ceased that activity.","keywords":["stop-ceasing"],"skill":"stop-ceasing","contextKey":"verb-pattern-meaning-50","fingerprint":"42fa372e64a234c32d085749c2cb64330ae6d4075ade996360c7bfd23082adff","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verb-meaning"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The activity itself **ended**; this is stop + -ing.","**Stopped repairing** means they ceased that activity.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Stop cannot take a bare base verb for this meaning.","B":"A past form cannot serve as this complement.","C":"**Stopped repairing** means they ceased that activity.","D":"Stopped to would mean paused another activity in order to do this one."}}},{"id":"712d53df-ffa4-5d00-aa88-dead4eac6ab6","topicSlug":"verb-pattern-meaning","level":"B2","prompt":"We interrupted our walk in order to do the task: “We stopped ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The blank answers **why we paused another activity**.","explanation":"**Stopped to repair** gives the purpose of pausing the walk.","keywords":["stop-purpose"],"skill":"stop-purpose","contextKey":"verb-pattern-meaning-50","fingerprint":"6ba9fa5a6511ec2a7743404e2417d1e0add78fa611397decacc630135d01113d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verb-meaning"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The blank answers **why we paused another activity**.","**Stopped to repair** gives the purpose of pausing the walk.","Choose **to repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Stopped + -ing would mean we ceased this task, not paused the walk to begin it.","B":"A purpose infinitive needs to.","C":"A past form does not express the purpose of stopping.","D":"**Stopped to repair** gives the purpose of pausing the walk."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Distinguish remembering a duty from recalling an experience, stopping an action from pausing for a purpose, and methods from attempts.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Remember a duty or recall an experience","explanation":"**Remember to do** means remembering a necessary action before doing it. **Remember doing** means recalling an earlier experience. The same contrast often appears with forget, although a negative such as I’ll never forget meeting her is especially common.","pattern":"Remember **to lock** the door. / I remember **locking** it.","commonMistakes":["The -ing form refers to the remembered experience; it is not simply an alternative spelling of the infinitive."],"examples":["The task is still ahead. Complete the reminder: “Please remember **to prepare** the visitor guide.”","The task is still ahead. Complete the reminder: “Please remember **to restore** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Stop one activity or pause for another","explanation":"**Stop doing** means cease that activity. **Stop to do** means interrupt another activity in order to do this one. The second pattern is an infinitive of purpose, so it answers why the person paused.","pattern":"We stopped **talking**. / We stopped **to talk**.","commonMistakes":["Stopped to smoke does not mean quit smoking; it means paused in order to smoke."],"examples":["The task happened yesterday and I recall doing it: “I remember **preparing** the visitor guide.”","The task happened yesterday and I recall doing it: “I remember **restoring** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Try a method or attempt a difficult action","explanation":"**Try doing** often suggests testing a method to see if it helps. **Try to do** emphasises making an attempt, which may fail. Some contexts allow both with little practical difference, so tasks need a clear intention.","pattern":"Try **restarting** it. / I tried **to lift** it but could not.","commonMistakes":["Do not assume that try + -ing always succeeded; it describes the method being tested."],"examples":["They ceased this activity completely: “They stopped **preparing** the visitor guide.”","They ceased this activity completely: “They stopped **restoring** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Go on and regret","explanation":"Go on doing continues the same activity; go on to do moves to a new activity. Regret doing looks back with regret, while regret to inform is a conventional way to introduce unwelcome information. Learn these as meaning-and-form pairs.","pattern":"She went on **speaking**. / She went on **to explain** the next step.","commonMistakes":["Not every verb accepts both patterns. Avoid transferring these contrasts to verbs such as enjoy, which normally takes -ing."],"examples":["We interrupted our walk in order to do the task: “We stopped **to prepare** the visitor guide.”","We interrupted our walk in order to do the task: “We stopped **to restore** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["Remember **to lock** the door. / I remember **locking** it.","We stopped **talking**. / We stopped **to talk**.","Try **restarting** it. / I tried **to lift** it but could not.","She went on **speaking**. / She went on **to explain** the next step."]},"stage":"5 · Complex sentences","prerequisites":["reflexive-reciprocal"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
